referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int selectionProofTotalReceipt(int[] readings) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {Set<Integer>sums=new TreeSet<>();class Walk {void visit(int i,int s){if(i==a.length){sums.add(s);return;}visit(i+1,s);visit(i+1,s+a[i]);}}new Walk().visit(0,0);out.addAll(sums);}
    int answer=0;for(int x:out)answer+=x;return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build subsetTotals

Choose any positions from `readings`, or choose none. Add the numbers at those positions. List every different total you can make, from smallest to largest. Choosing no positions gives a total of 0. Each position can be chosen at most once. Equal numbers at different positions are separate choices, but each total appears only once in `subsetTotals`.

Return the answer

Add up every number in `subsetTotals`. Store the sum in `total`. Complete `selectionProofTotalReceipt` so it returns `total`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `subsetTotals` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n*2^n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(2^n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

